{:edb/db-before-t 2185 :edb/db-after-t 2186 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535498 50 #inst "2026-10-09T01:15:45.116Z" 13194139535498 true] [17592186054486 1100 "Let $f(x) = 3 x^4 - 5 x^3 - 3 x^2 + 6 x - 8$. If $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are zeros of $f(x)$, factor $f(x)$ completely." 13194139535498 false] [17592186054486 1100 "Let $f(x) = 3 x^4 - 5 x^3 - 3 x^2 + 6 x - 8.$ If $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are zeros of $f(x),$ factor $f(x)$ completely." 13194139535498 true] [17592186054486 1145 "Because $x=2$ and\n\n$$\nx=-\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$\n\nare both zeros of $f(x)$, the factor theorem guarantees that $(x-2)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nare both factors of $f(x)$. So, $(x-2)$ and\n\n$$\n\\left(x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nboth divide $f(x)$.\n\nTo begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by one of these factors (it doesn't matter which one we divide first). Let's divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= 3x^{4} - 5x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 6x - 8 \\\\\n&= (x - 2)(3x^{3} + x^{2} - x + 4)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, because\n\n$$\n\\left(x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nis also a factor of $f(x)$, it must be a factor of the quotient $3x^3 + x^2 - x +4$. So, we divide this quotient by\n\n$$\n\\left(x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)\n$$\n\nusing synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{3} + x^{2} - x + 4 &= (x + \\frac{4}{3})(3x^{2} - 3x + 3) \\\\\n&= (x + \\frac{4}{3})(3)(x^{2} - x + 1) \\\\\n&= (3x + 4)(x^{2} - x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x^{4} - 5x^{3} - 3x^{2} + 6x - 8 &= (x - 2)(3x + 4)(x^{2} - x + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic polynomial $x^2-x+1$ does not appear to factor over the real numbers. We can make sure of this by verifying that its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\nD = b^{2} - 4ac = (-1)^{2} - 4(1)(1) =-3 < 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-2)(3x+4)(x^2-x+1)\n$$" 13194139535498 false] [17592186054486 1145 "Because $x=2$ and $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ are both zeros of $f(x),$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x-2)$ and $\\left( x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ are both factors of $f(x).$ So, $(x-2)$ and $\\left( x + \\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ both divide $f(x).$\n\nTo begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by one of these factors (it doesn't matter which one we divide first). Let's divide $f(x)$ by $(x-2)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}2$ | $3$ | $-5$ | $-3$ | $6$ | $-8$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $6$ | $2$ | $-2$ | $8$ |\n|  | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-8 \\\\  & =(x-2)(3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, because $\\left( x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $3x^3 + x^2 - x +4.$ So, we divide this quotient by $\\left( x+\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-\\dfrac{4}{3}$ | $3$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-4$ | $4$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $3$ | $-3$ | $3$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-x+4 & =(x+\\frac{4}{3})(3{x}^{2}-3x+3) \\\\  & =(x+\\frac{4}{3})(3)({x}^{2}-x+1) \\\\  & =(3x+4)({x}^{2}-x+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-8 & =(x-2)(3x+4)({x}^{2}-x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe quadratic polynomial $x^2-x+1$ does not appear to factor over the real numbers. We can make sure of this by verifying that its discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D={b}^{2}-4ac=(-1)^{2}-4(1)(1)=-3<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full factorization of $f(x)$ over the real numbers is given by \n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-2)(3x+4)(x^2-x+1).\n$$" 13194139535498 true] [17592186054487 1100 "Given that $(x-1)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of the polynomial $f(x)= x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6$, factor $f(x)$ completely." 13194139535498 false] [17592186054487 1100 "Given that $(x-1)$ and $(x-3)$ are factors of the polynomial $f(x)= x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6,$ factor $f(x)$ completely." 13194139535498 true] [17592186054487 1145 "To factor $f(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x)$, we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6}{x-1} = x^3-7x-6\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x^3-7x-6)\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x)$, it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3-7x-6)$. So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3} = x^2+3x +2\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x-3) (x^2+3x +2)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x-3) (x+2)(x+1)\n$$" 13194139535498 false] [17592186054487 1145 "To factor $f(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $f(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $f(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}1$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-7$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6}{x-1} = x^3-7x-6,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 = (x-1) (x^3-7x-6).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x-3)$ is also a factor of $f(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3-7x-6).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x-3)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}3$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $3$ | $9$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3-7x-6}{x-3} = x^2+3x +2,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x^2+3x +2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 7 x^2 + x + 6 =  (x-1) (x-3) (x+2)(x+1).\n$$" 13194139535498 true] [17592186054488 1100 "Let $p(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4$. Knowing that $p(4) = p(-1) = 0$, factor the polynomial $p(x)$ completely." 13194139535498 false] [17592186054488 1100 "Let $p(x) = x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4.$ Knowing that $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ factor the polynomial $p(x)$ completely." 13194139535498 true] [17592186054488 1145 "Since\n\n$$\np(4) = p(-1) = 0\n$$\n\nthe factor theorem guarantees that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are both factors of $p(x)$.\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $p(x)$, we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x)$, we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-9$ | $-11$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $12$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4}{x-4} = x^3+3x^2+3x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4) (x^3+3x^2+3x+1)\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x)$, it must be a factor of the quotient $(x^3+3x^2+3x+1)$. So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^3+3x^2+3x-1}{x+1} = x^2+2x+1\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4)(x+1)(x^2+2x+1)\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{4} - x^{3} - 9x^{2} - 11x - 4 &= (x - 4)(x + 1)(x + 1)^{2} \\\\\n&= (x - 4)(x + 1)^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535498 false] [17592186054488 1145 "Since $p(4) = p(-1) = 0,$ the factor theorem guarantees that $(x - 4)$ and $(x + 1)$ are both factors of $p(x).$\nTo factor $p(x)$ completely, we need to find its other factors.\n\nSince $(x - 4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x - 4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x - 4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n|  | $x^4$ | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}4$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $-9$ | $-11$ | $-4$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $4$ | $12$ | $12$ | $4$ |\n|  | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4}{x-4} = x^3+3x^2+3x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4) (  x^3+3x^2+3x+1).\n$$\n\nNow, because $(x+1)$ is also a factor of $p(x),$ it must be a factor of the quotient $( x^3+3x^2+3x+1).$ So, we divide this quotient by $(x+1)$ using synthetic division once more:\n\n|  | $x^3$ | $x^2$ | $x^1$ | $x^0$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\color{red}-1$ | $1$ | $3$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n|  | $\\color{gray}\\downarrow$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-1$ |\n|  | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $0$ |\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ x^3+3x^2+3x-1}{x+1} = x^2+2x+1,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx^4 - x^3 - 9 x^2 - 11 x - 4 = (x-4)(x+1)(x^2+2x+1).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}-11x-4 & =(x-4)(x+1)(x+1)^{2} \\\\  & =(x-4)(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535498 true] [17592186054716 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352188 13194139535498 true] [17592186054716 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352195 13194139535498 true] [17592186054716 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352202 13194139535498 true] [17592186054716 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352230 13194139535498 true] [17592186054716 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352260 13194139535498 true] [17592186054717 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352209 13194139535498 true] [17592186054717 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352237 13194139535498 true] [17592186054717 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352247 13194139535498 true] [17592186054717 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352264 13194139535498 true] [17592186054717 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352277 13194139535498 true] [17592186054718 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352216 13194139535498 true] [17592186054718 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352223 13194139535498 true] [17592186054718 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352257 13194139535498 true] [17592186054718 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352273 13194139535498 true] [17592186054718 1020 #edb/ref 17592186352286 13194139535498 true] [17592186352188 1017 #edb/ref 17592186352189 13194139535498 true] [17592186352188 1089 "q-112615" 13194139535498 tr